{"success": true, "name": "interactive-arc-evaluation", "file": "references/recurrence-notes.md", "content": "# Handling unresolved task recurrences

A recurrence may have extensive historical notes describing failed attempts and an unsolved rule. Treat those notes as cautionary background, not as proof that the new instance is unsolvable or that prior hypotheses have been tested on its fresh input. Restart analysis from the new instance's demos and current budget.

For every demo, map exact changed cells/objects and compare unchanged near-matches before requesting more evidence. State what uncertainty the next demo is intended to reduce. Avoid spending action turns on non-evidence activity. With one observed demo, a single transformed component typically does not establish a selector; retain uncertainty and use another carefully chosen demo or later evidence instead of extrapolating.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790682173/attempts/6cfc9d2b2dd84862bee2cc238bdc875a/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/interactive-arc-evaluation/references/recurrence-notes.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/failed-target-inference.md", "content": "# Avoiding unsupported target guesses

During motif-copy ARC tasks, a visually plausible output can still be wrong if target identification is guessed from a generic property such as “all isolated zeros.” Build the rule from the demos' exact delta (output minus input): record every changed coordinate, cluster the changed cells into footprints, and compare each footprint with candidate translations of the source motif. Validate the rule against all outputs, including unchanged near-matches, before applying it to the test.

